----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, N: b1-d2, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, Q: d1-d2, Q: d1-e1, Q: d1-e2, Q: d1-f3, Q: d1-g4, Q: d1-h5, B: d3-e2, B: d3-c4, B: d3-b5, B: d3-a6, B: d3-e4, P: d4-e5, R: f1-e1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: Let me analyze the current position. I'm playing as White and need to assess the key features:\n\n**Material Count:**\n- White is down significantly in material - Black has both knights on the board (e4, e5) and a bishop on f5, while I appear to have lost my king.\n- Wait, I need to recheck - looking at row 1, I see KWF1 at g1, so my king is still there.\n\n**Position Analysis:**\n- Black has advanced pieces: two knights on e4 and e5, and a bishop on f5 - these are well-placed in the center\n- Black's d5 pawn is advanced and controls key central squares\n- My bishop on d3 is somewhat active\n- My king has castled kingside (evidenced by KWF1 at g1 and RWF1 at f1)\n\n**Tactical Opportunities:**\n1. **d4xe5** - My d4 pawn can capture Black's knight on e5. This would win a knight (worth ~3 points), a significant material gain.\n2. **B: d3-e4** - My bishop could capture the knight on e4, but then Black's d5 pawn could recapture (d5xe4).\n3. **Q: d1-h5** - Aggressive queen move attacking f7 and h7, but Black's king is relatively safe.\n4. **P: f2-f3** - Attacks the knight on e4, forcing it to move.\n\n**Best Move Analysis:**\nThe most forcing and materially advantageous move is **P: d4-e5**, capturing the knight on e5. This:\n- Wins a knight immediately (3 points of material)\n- Opens up lines for my pieces\n- The knight on e5 is undefended\n- Black cannot recapture effectively\n\nReason: Capturing the undefended knight on e5 with my d4 pawn wins significant material (a whole knight) and improves my position. This is a clear tactical win that Black cannot prevent, and material advantage is crucial in chess.\n\nAnswer: P: d4-e5"}
